window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2019-06-3", "source": "2019年06月真题·第3套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay on the importance of motivation and methods in learning. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "In the journey of learning, two elements stand out as indispensable: motivation and methods. Motivation\nsupplies the inner drive to keep going, while sound methods ensure that our efforts actually pay off. In my\nview, neither can be neglected if we hope to learn well.\nOn the one hand, motivation is the engine of learning. It gives us a clear direction and the persistence to push\nthrough difficulties. A student who longs to become a doctor, for instance, will happily review anatomy late\ninto the night, whereas one without any goal soon gives up. On the other hand, effective methods make\nlearning far more efficient. By planning our time, taking notes and reviewing regularly, we absorb more in less\ntime; blind hard work, by contrast, often wastes precious hours.\nIn conclusion, motivation and methods are equally important and mutually reinforcing: motivation is the\nengine that starts the journey, and methods are the road that leads to the destination. For us students, the wise\nchoice is to fuel our passion while constantly refining how we study."}, "translation": {"prompt": "汉语现在是世界上用作本族语人数最多的语言。汉语与西方语言的一个重要区别在于它是以方块字(character)而不是以字母构成的。目前仍在使用的书写系统中，汉语是最古老的。在中国，来自不同地区的人可能听不懂对方的方言，但由于汉子有统一的书写形式，他们交流起来几乎没有任何困难。汉语历史上对团结中华民族发挥了重要作用。今天，随着中国经济的快速增长和全球影响力的增强，越来越多其他国家的人也开始学习汉语。", "sample": "Chinese is now the language spoken by the largest number of people as their native language in the world. An important difference between Chinese and Western languages lies in that it is composed of characters rather than letters. Among the writing systems still in use today, Chinese is the oldest. In China, people from different regions may not understand each other's dialects, but since Chinese characters share a unified written form, they have almost no difficulty communicating with one another. Throughout history, Chinese has played an important role in uniting the Chinese nation. Today, with the rapid growth of China's economy and the increase of its global influence, more and more people from other countries are beginning to learn Chinese."}, "listeningUrl": null, "clozePassage": "Steel is valued for its reliability, but not when it gets cold. Most forms of steel  __26__  become brittle(脆的) at temperatures below about -25℃ unless they are mixed with other metals. Now, though, a novel type of steel has been developed that resists  __27__  at much lower temperatures, while retaining its strength and toughness—without the need for expensive  __28__  .\nSteel's fragility at low temperatures first became a major concern during the Second World War. After German U-boats torpedoed(用鱼雷攻击) numerous British ships, a 2700-strong fleet of cheap-and-cheerful \"Liberty ships\" was introduced to replace the lost vessels, providing a lifeline for the  __29__  British. But the steel shells of hundreds of the ships  __30__  in the icy North Atlantic, and 12 broke in half and sank.\nBrittleness remains a problem when building steel structures in cold conditions, such as oil rigs in the Arctic. So scientists have  __31__  to find a solution by mixing it with expensive metals such as nickel.\nYuuji Kimura and colleagues in Japan tried a more physical  __32__  . Rather than adding other metals, they developed a complex mechanical process involving repeated heating and very severe mechanical deformation, known as temp forming.\nThe resulting steel appears to achieve a combination of strength and toughness that is  __33__  to that of modern steels that are very rich in alloy content and, therefore, very expensive.\nKimura's team intends to use its tempformed steel to make ultra-high strength parts, such as bolts. They hope to reduce both the number of  __34__  needed in a construction job and their weight—by replacing solid supports with  __35__  tubes, for example. This could reduce the amount of steel needed to make everything from automobiles to buildings and bridges.", "matchIntro": "The future of personal satellite technology is here—are we ready for it?", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Satellites used to be the exclusive playthings of rich governments and wealthy corporations. But increasingly, as space becomes more democratized, they are coming within reach of ordinary people. Just like drones(无人机) before them, miniature satellites are beginning to fundamentally transform our conceptions of who gets to do what up above our heads."}, {"label": "B", "text": "As a recent report from the National Academy of Sciences highlights, these satellites hold tremendous potential for making satellite-based science more accessible than ever before. However, as the cost of getting your own satellite in orbit drops sharply, the risks of irresponsible use grow. The question here is no longer \"Can we?\" but \"Should we?\" What are the potential downsides of having a slice of space densely populated by equipment built by people not traditionally labeled as \"professionals\"? And what would the responsible and beneficial development and use of this technology actually look like? Some of the answers may come from a nonprofit organization that has been building and launching amateur satellites for nearly 50 years."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Having your personal satellite launched into orbit might sound like an idea straight out of science fiction. But over the past few decades a unique class of satellites has been created that fits the bill: CubeSats. The \"Cube\" here simply refers to the satellite's shape. The most common CubeSat is a 10cm cube, so small that a single CubeSat could easily be mistaken for a paperweight on your desk. These mini-satellites can fit in a launch vehicle's formerly \"wasted space.\" Multiples can be deployed in combination for more complex missions than could be achieved by one CubeSat alone."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Within their compact bodies these minute satellites are able to house sensors and communications receivers/transmitters that enable operators to study Earth from space, as well as space around Earth. They're primarily designed for Low Earth Orbit (LEO)—an easily accessible region of space from around 200 to 800 miles above Earth, where human-tended missions like the Hubble Space Telescope and the International Space Station (ISS) hang out. But they can attain more distant orbits; NASA plans for most of its future Earth-escaping payloads (to the moon and Mars especially) to carry CubeSats."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Because they're so small and light, it costs much less to get a CubeSat into Earth's orbit than a traditional communications or GPS satellite. For instance, a research group here at Arizona State University recently claimed their developmental small CubeSats could cost as little as $3,000 to put in orbit. This decrease in cost allows researchers, hobbyists and even elementary school groups to put simple instruments into LEO or even have them deployed from the ISS."}, {"label": "F", "text": "The first CubeSat was created in the early 2000s, as a way of enabling Stanford graduate students to design, build, test and operate a spacecraft with similar capabilities to the USSR's Sputnik(前苏联的人造卫星). Since then, NASA, the National Reconnaissance Office and even Boeing have all launched and operated CubeSats. There are more than 130 currently in operation. The NASA Educational Launch of Nano Satellite program, which offers free launches for educational groups and science missions, is now open to U.S. nonprofit corporations as well. Clearly, satellites are not just for rocket scientists anymore."}, {"label": "G", "text": "The National Academy of Sciences report emphasizes CubeSats' importance in scientific discovery and the training of future space scientists and engineers. Yet it also acknowledges that widespread deployment of LEO CubeSats isn't risk-free. The greatest concern the authors raise is space debris—pieces of \"junk\" that orbit the earth, with the potential to cause serious damage if they collide with operational units, including the ISS."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Currently, there aren't many CubeSats and they're tracked closely. Yet as LEO opens up to more amateur satellites, they may pose an increasing threat. As the report authors point out, even near-misses might lead to the \"creation of a burdensome regulatory framework and affect the future disposition of science CubeSats.\""}, {"label": "I", "text": "CubeSat researchers suggest that now's the time to ponder unexpected and unintended possible consequences of more people than ever having access to their own small slice of space. In an era when you can simply buy a CubeSat kit off the shelf, how can we trust the satellites over our heads were developed with good intentions by people who knew what they were doing? Some \"expert amateurs\" in the satellite game could provide some inspiration for how to proceed responsibly."}, {"label": "J", "text": "In 1969, the Radio Amateur Satellite Corporation (AMSAT) was created in order to foster ham radio enthusiasts'(业余无线电爱好者) participation in space research and communication. It continued the efforts, begun in 1961, by Project OSCAR—a U.S.-based group that built and launched the very first nongovernmental satellite just four years after Sputnik. As an organization of volunteers, AMSAT was putting \"amateur\" satellites in orbit decades before the current CubeSat craze. And over time, its members have learned a thing or two about responsibility. Here, open-source development has been a central principle. Within the organization, AMSAT has a philosophy of open sourcing everything making technical data on all aspects of their satellites fully available to everyone in the organization, and when possible, the public. According to a member of the team responsible for FOX 1-A, AMSAT's first CubeSat, this means that there's no way to sneak something like explosives or an energy emitter into an amateur satellite when everyone has access to the designs and implementation."}, {"label": "K", "text": "However, they're more cautious about sharing information with nonmembers, as the organization guards against others developing the ability to hijack and take control of their satellites. This form of \"self-governance\" is possible within long-standing amateur organizations that, over time, are able to build a sense of responsibility to community members, as well as society in general. But what happens when new players emerge, who don't have deep roots within the existing culture?"}, {"label": "L", "text": "Hobbyists and students are gaining access to technologies without being part of a long-standing amateur establishment. They're still constrained by funders, launch providers and a series of regulations—all of which rein in what CubeSat developers can and cannot do. But there's a danger they're ill-equipped to think through potential unintended consequences. What these unintended consequences might be is admittedly far from clear. Yet we know innovators can be remarkably creative with taking technologies in unexpected directions. Think of something as seemingly benign as the cellphone—we have microfinance and text-based social networking at one end of the spectrum, and improvised(临时制作的) explosive devices at the other."}, {"label": "M", "text": "This is where a culture of social responsibility around CubeSats becomes important – not simply to ensure that physical risks are minimized, but to engage with a much larger community in anticipating and managing less obvious consequences of the technology. This is not an easy task. Yet the evidence from AMSAT and other areas of technology development suggests that responsible amateur communities can and do emerge around novel technologies. The challenge here, of course, is ensuring that what an amateur community considers to be responsible, actually is. Here's where there needs to be a much wider public conversation that extends beyond government agencies and scientific communities to include students, hobbyists, and anyone who may potentially stand to be affected by the use of CubeSat technology."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "When I re-entered the full-time workforce a few years ago after a decade of solitary self-employment, there was one thing I was looking forward to the most: the opportunity to have work friends once again. It wasn't until I entered the corporate world that I realized, for me at least, being friends with colleagues didn't emerge as a priority at all. This is surprising when you consider the prevailing emphasis by scholars and trainers and managers on the importance of cultivating close interpersonal relationships at work. So much research has explored the way in which collegial(同事的) ties can help overcome a range of workplace issues affecting productivity and the quality of work output such as team-based conflict, jealousy, undermining, anger, and more.\nPerhaps my expectations of lunches, water-cooler gossip and caring, deep-and-meaningful conversations were a legacy of the last time I was in that kind of office environment. Whereas now, as I near the end of my fourth decade, I realize work can be fully functional and entirely fulfilling without needing to be best mates with the people sitting next to you.\nIn an academic analysis just published in the profoundly-respected Journal of Management, researchers have looked at the concept of \"indifferent relationships\". It's a simple term that encapsulates(概括) the fact that relationships at work can reasonably be non-intimate, inconsequential, unimportant and even, dare I say it, disposable or substitutable.\nIndifferent relationships are neither positive nor negative. The limited research conducted thus far indicates they're especially dominant among those who value independence over cooperation, and harmony over confrontation. Indifference is also the preferred option among those who are socially lazy. Maintaining relationships over the long term takes effort. For some of us, too much effort.\nAs noted above, indifferent relationships may not always be the most helpful approach in resolving some of the issues that pop up at work. But there are nonetheless several empirically proven benefits. One of those is efficiency. Less time chatting and socializing means more time working and churning(产出).\nThe other is self-esteem. As human beings, we're primed to compare ourselves to each other in what is an anxiety-inducing phenomenon. Apparently, we look down on acquaintances more so than friends. Since the former is most common among those inclined towards indifferent relationships, their predominance can bolster individuals' sense of self-worth.\nEgo aside, a third advantage is that the emotional neutrality of indifferent relationships has been found to enhance critical evaluation, to strengthen one's focus on task resolution, and to gain greater access to valuable information. None of that might be as fun as after-work socializing but, hey, I'll take it anyway.", "47": "When I re-entered the full-time workforce a few years ago after a decade of solitary self-employment, there was one thing I was looking forward to the most: the opportunity to have work friends once again. It wasn't until I entered the corporate world that I realized, for me at least, being friends with colleagues didn't emerge as a priority at all. This is surprising when you consider the prevailing emphasis by scholars and trainers and managers on the importance of cultivating close interpersonal relationships at work. So much research has explored the way in which collegial(同事的) ties can help overcome a range of workplace issues affecting productivity and the quality of work output such as team-based conflict, jealousy, undermining, anger, and more.\nPerhaps my expectations of lunches, water-cooler gossip and caring, deep-and-meaningful conversations were a legacy of the last time I was in that kind of office environment. Whereas now, as I near the end of my fourth decade, I realize work can be fully functional and entirely fulfilling without needing to be best mates with the people sitting next to you.\nIn an academic analysis just published in the profoundly-respected Journal of Management, researchers have looked at the concept of \"indifferent relationships\". It's a simple term that encapsulates(概括) the fact that relationships at work can reasonably be non-intimate, inconsequential, unimportant and even, dare I say it, disposable or substitutable.\nIndifferent relationships are neither positive nor negative. The limited research conducted thus far indicates they're especially dominant among those who value independence over cooperation, and harmony over confrontation. Indifference is also the preferred option among those who are socially lazy. Maintaining relationships over the long term takes effort. For some of us, too much effort.\nAs noted above, indifferent relationships may not always be the most helpful approach in resolving some of the issues that pop up at work. But there are nonetheless several empirically proven benefits. One of those is efficiency. Less time chatting and socializing means more time working and churning(产出).\nThe other is self-esteem. As human beings, we're primed to compare ourselves to each other in what is an anxiety-inducing phenomenon. Apparently, we look down on acquaintances more so than friends. Since the former is most common among those inclined towards indifferent relationships, their predominance can bolster individuals' sense of self-worth.\nEgo aside, a third advantage is that the emotional neutrality of indifferent relationships has been found to enhance critical evaluation, to strengthen one's focus on task resolution, and to gain greater access to valuable information. None of that might be as fun as after-work socializing but, hey, I'll take it anyway.", "48": "When I re-entered the full-time workforce a few years ago after a decade of solitary self-employment, there was one thing I was looking forward to the most: the opportunity to have work friends once again. It wasn't until I entered the corporate world that I realized, for me at least, being friends with colleagues didn't emerge as a priority at all. This is surprising when you consider the prevailing emphasis by scholars and trainers and managers on the importance of cultivating close interpersonal relationships at work. So much research has explored the way in which collegial(同事的) ties can help overcome a range of workplace issues affecting productivity and the quality of work output such as team-based conflict, jealousy, undermining, anger, and more.\nPerhaps my expectations of lunches, water-cooler gossip and caring, deep-and-meaningful conversations were a legacy of the last time I was in that kind of office environment. Whereas now, as I near the end of my fourth decade, I realize work can be fully functional and entirely fulfilling without needing to be best mates with the people sitting next to you.\nIn an academic analysis just published in the profoundly-respected Journal of Management, researchers have looked at the concept of \"indifferent relationships\". It's a simple term that encapsulates(概括) the fact that relationships at work can reasonably be non-intimate, inconsequential, unimportant and even, dare I say it, disposable or substitutable.\nIndifferent relationships are neither positive nor negative. The limited research conducted thus far indicates they're especially dominant among those who value independence over cooperation, and harmony over confrontation. Indifference is also the preferred option among those who are socially lazy. Maintaining relationships over the long term takes effort. For some of us, too much effort.\nAs noted above, indifferent relationships may not always be the most helpful approach in resolving some of the issues that pop up at work. But there are nonetheless several empirically proven benefits. One of those is efficiency. Less time chatting and socializing means more time working and churning(产出).\nThe other is self-esteem. As human beings, we're primed to compare ourselves to each other in what is an anxiety-inducing phenomenon. Apparently, we look down on acquaintances more so than friends. Since the former is most common among those inclined towards indifferent relationships, their predominance can bolster individuals' sense of self-worth.\nEgo aside, a third advantage is that the emotional neutrality of indifferent relationships has been found to enhance critical evaluation, to strengthen one's focus on task resolution, and to gain greater access to valuable information. None of that might be as fun as after-work socializing but, hey, I'll take it anyway.", "49": "When I re-entered the full-time workforce a few years ago after a decade of solitary self-employment, there was one thing I was looking forward to the most: the opportunity to have work friends once again. It wasn't until I entered the corporate world that I realized, for me at least, being friends with colleagues didn't emerge as a priority at all. This is surprising when you consider the prevailing emphasis by scholars and trainers and managers on the importance of cultivating close interpersonal relationships at work. So much research has explored the way in which collegial(同事的) ties can help overcome a range of workplace issues affecting productivity and the quality of work output such as team-based conflict, jealousy, undermining, anger, and more.\nPerhaps my expectations of lunches, water-cooler gossip and caring, deep-and-meaningful conversations were a legacy of the last time I was in that kind of office environment. Whereas now, as I near the end of my fourth decade, I realize work can be fully functional and entirely fulfilling without needing to be best mates with the people sitting next to you.\nIn an academic analysis just published in the profoundly-respected Journal of Management, researchers have looked at the concept of \"indifferent relationships\". It's a simple term that encapsulates(概括) the fact that relationships at work can reasonably be non-intimate, inconsequential, unimportant and even, dare I say it, disposable or substitutable.\nIndifferent relationships are neither positive nor negative. The limited research conducted thus far indicates they're especially dominant among those who value independence over cooperation, and harmony over confrontation. Indifference is also the preferred option among those who are socially lazy. Maintaining relationships over the long term takes effort. For some of us, too much effort.\nAs noted above, indifferent relationships may not always be the most helpful approach in resolving some of the issues that pop up at work. But there are nonetheless several empirically proven benefits. One of those is efficiency. Less time chatting and socializing means more time working and churning(产出).\nThe other is self-esteem. As human beings, we're primed to compare ourselves to each other in what is an anxiety-inducing phenomenon. Apparently, we look down on acquaintances more so than friends. Since the former is most common among those inclined towards indifferent relationships, their predominance can bolster individuals' sense of self-worth.\nEgo aside, a third advantage is that the emotional neutrality of indifferent relationships has been found to enhance critical evaluation, to strengthen one's focus on task resolution, and to gain greater access to valuable information. None of that might be as fun as after-work socializing but, hey, I'll take it anyway.", "50": "When I re-entered the full-time workforce a few years ago after a decade of solitary self-employment, there was one thing I was looking forward to the most: the opportunity to have work friends once again. It wasn't until I entered the corporate world that I realized, for me at least, being friends with colleagues didn't emerge as a priority at all. This is surprising when you consider the prevailing emphasis by scholars and trainers and managers on the importance of cultivating close interpersonal relationships at work. So much research has explored the way in which collegial(同事的) ties can help overcome a range of workplace issues affecting productivity and the quality of work output such as team-based conflict, jealousy, undermining, anger, and more.\nPerhaps my expectations of lunches, water-cooler gossip and caring, deep-and-meaningful conversations were a legacy of the last time I was in that kind of office environment. Whereas now, as I near the end of my fourth decade, I realize work can be fully functional and entirely fulfilling without needing to be best mates with the people sitting next to you.\nIn an academic analysis just published in the profoundly-respected Journal of Management, researchers have looked at the concept of \"indifferent relationships\". It's a simple term that encapsulates(概括) the fact that relationships at work can reasonably be non-intimate, inconsequential, unimportant and even, dare I say it, disposable or substitutable.\nIndifferent relationships are neither positive nor negative. The limited research conducted thus far indicates they're especially dominant among those who value independence over cooperation, and harmony over confrontation. Indifference is also the preferred option among those who are socially lazy. Maintaining relationships over the long term takes effort. For some of us, too much effort.\nAs noted above, indifferent relationships may not always be the most helpful approach in resolving some of the issues that pop up at work. But there are nonetheless several empirically proven benefits. One of those is efficiency. Less time chatting and socializing means more time working and churning(产出).\nThe other is self-esteem. As human beings, we're primed to compare ourselves to each other in what is an anxiety-inducing phenomenon. Apparently, we look down on acquaintances more so than friends. Since the former is most common among those inclined towards indifferent relationships, their predominance can bolster individuals' sense of self-worth.\nEgo aside, a third advantage is that the emotional neutrality of indifferent relationships has been found to enhance critical evaluation, to strengthen one's focus on task resolution, and to gain greater access to valuable information. None of that might be as fun as after-work socializing but, hey, I'll take it anyway.", "51": "In a few decades, artificial intelligence (AI) will surpass many of the abilities that we believe make us special. This is a grand challenge for our age and it may require an \"irrational\" response.\nOne of the most significant pieces of news from the US in early 2017 was the efforts of Google to make autonomous driving a reality. According to a report, Google's self-driving cars clocked 1,023,330 km, and required human intervention 124 times. That is one intervention about every 8,047 km of autonomous driving. But even more impressive is the progress in just a single year: human interventions fell from 0.8 times per thousand miles to 0.2, a 400% improvement. With such progress, Google's cars will easily surpass my own driving ability later this year.\nDriving once seemed to be a very human skill. But we said that about chess, too. Then a computer beat the human world champion, repeatedly. The board game Go(围棋) took over from chess as a new test for human thinking in 2016, when a computer beat one of the world's leading professional Go players. With computers conquering what used to be deeply human tasks, what will it mean in the future to be human? I worry about my six-year-old son. What will his place be in a world where machines beat us in one area after another? He'll never calculate faster, never drive better, or even fly more safely. Actually, it all comes down to a fairly simple question: What's so special about us? It can't be skills like arithmetic, which machines already excel in. So far, machines have a pretty hard time emulating creativity, arbitrary enough not to be predicted by a computer, and yet more than simple randomness.\nPerhaps, if we continue to improve information-processing machines, we'll soon have helpful rational assistants. So we must aim to complement the rationality of the machine, rather than to compete with it. If I'm right, we should foster a creative spirit because a dose of illogical creativity will complement the rationality of the machine. Unfortunately, however, our education system has not caught up to the approaching reality. Indeed, our schools and universities are structured to mould pupils to be mostly obedient servants of rationality, and to develop outdated skills in interacting with outdated machines. We need to help our children learn how to best work with smart computers to improve human decision-making. But most of all we need to keep the long-term perspective in mind: that even if computers will outsmart us, we can still be the most creative. Because if we aren't, we won't be providing much value in future ecosystems, and that may put in question the foundation for our existence.", "52": "In a few decades, artificial intelligence (AI) will surpass many of the abilities that we believe make us special. This is a grand challenge for our age and it may require an \"irrational\" response.\nOne of the most significant pieces of news from the US in early 2017 was the efforts of Google to make autonomous driving a reality. According to a report, Google's self-driving cars clocked 1,023,330 km, and required human intervention 124 times. That is one intervention about every 8,047 km of autonomous driving. But even more impressive is the progress in just a single year: human interventions fell from 0.8 times per thousand miles to 0.2, a 400% improvement. With such progress, Google's cars will easily surpass my own driving ability later this year.\nDriving once seemed to be a very human skill. But we said that about chess, too. Then a computer beat the human world champion, repeatedly. The board game Go(围棋) took over from chess as a new test for human thinking in 2016, when a computer beat one of the world's leading professional Go players. With computers conquering what used to be deeply human tasks, what will it mean in the future to be human? I worry about my six-year-old son. What will his place be in a world where machines beat us in one area after another? He'll never calculate faster, never drive better, or even fly more safely. Actually, it all comes down to a fairly simple question: What's so special about us? It can't be skills like arithmetic, which machines already excel in. So far, machines have a pretty hard time emulating creativity, arbitrary enough not to be predicted by a computer, and yet more than simple randomness.\nPerhaps, if we continue to improve information-processing machines, we'll soon have helpful rational assistants. So we must aim to complement the rationality of the machine, rather than to compete with it. If I'm right, we should foster a creative spirit because a dose of illogical creativity will complement the rationality of the machine. Unfortunately, however, our education system has not caught up to the approaching reality. Indeed, our schools and universities are structured to mould pupils to be mostly obedient servants of rationality, and to develop outdated skills in interacting with outdated machines. We need to help our children learn how to best work with smart computers to improve human decision-making. But most of all we need to keep the long-term perspective in mind: that even if computers will outsmart us, we can still be the most creative. Because if we aren't, we won't be providing much value in future ecosystems, and that may put in question the foundation for our existence.", "53": "In a few decades, artificial intelligence (AI) will surpass many of the abilities that we believe make us special. This is a grand challenge for our age and it may require an \"irrational\" response.\nOne of the most significant pieces of news from the US in early 2017 was the efforts of Google to make autonomous driving a reality. According to a report, Google's self-driving cars clocked 1,023,330 km, and required human intervention 124 times. That is one intervention about every 8,047 km of autonomous driving. But even more impressive is the progress in just a single year: human interventions fell from 0.8 times per thousand miles to 0.2, a 400% improvement. With such progress, Google's cars will easily surpass my own driving ability later this year.\nDriving once seemed to be a very human skill. But we said that about chess, too. Then a computer beat the human world champion, repeatedly. The board game Go(围棋) took over from chess as a new test for human thinking in 2016, when a computer beat one of the world's leading professional Go players. With computers conquering what used to be deeply human tasks, what will it mean in the future to be human? I worry about my six-year-old son. What will his place be in a world where machines beat us in one area after another? He'll never calculate faster, never drive better, or even fly more safely. Actually, it all comes down to a fairly simple question: What's so special about us? It can't be skills like arithmetic, which machines already excel in. So far, machines have a pretty hard time emulating creativity, arbitrary enough not to be predicted by a computer, and yet more than simple randomness.\nPerhaps, if we continue to improve information-processing machines, we'll soon have helpful rational assistants. So we must aim to complement the rationality of the machine, rather than to compete with it. If I'm right, we should foster a creative spirit because a dose of illogical creativity will complement the rationality of the machine. Unfortunately, however, our education system has not caught up to the approaching reality. Indeed, our schools and universities are structured to mould pupils to be mostly obedient servants of rationality, and to develop outdated skills in interacting with outdated machines. We need to help our children learn how to best work with smart computers to improve human decision-making. But most of all we need to keep the long-term perspective in mind: that even if computers will outsmart us, we can still be the most creative. Because if we aren't, we won't be providing much value in future ecosystems, and that may put in question the foundation for our existence.", "54": "In a few decades, artificial intelligence (AI) will surpass many of the abilities that we believe make us special. This is a grand challenge for our age and it may require an \"irrational\" response.\nOne of the most significant pieces of news from the US in early 2017 was the efforts of Google to make autonomous driving a reality. According to a report, Google's self-driving cars clocked 1,023,330 km, and required human intervention 124 times. That is one intervention about every 8,047 km of autonomous driving. But even more impressive is the progress in just a single year: human interventions fell from 0.8 times per thousand miles to 0.2, a 400% improvement. With such progress, Google's cars will easily surpass my own driving ability later this year.\nDriving once seemed to be a very human skill. But we said that about chess, too. Then a computer beat the human world champion, repeatedly. The board game Go(围棋) took over from chess as a new test for human thinking in 2016, when a computer beat one of the world's leading professional Go players. With computers conquering what used to be deeply human tasks, what will it mean in the future to be human? I worry about my six-year-old son. What will his place be in a world where machines beat us in one area after another? He'll never calculate faster, never drive better, or even fly more safely. Actually, it all comes down to a fairly simple question: What's so special about us? It can't be skills like arithmetic, which machines already excel in. So far, machines have a pretty hard time emulating creativity, arbitrary enough not to be predicted by a computer, and yet more than simple randomness.\nPerhaps, if we continue to improve information-processing machines, we'll soon have helpful rational assistants. So we must aim to complement the rationality of the machine, rather than to compete with it. If I'm right, we should foster a creative spirit because a dose of illogical creativity will complement the rationality of the machine. Unfortunately, however, our education system has not caught up to the approaching reality. Indeed, our schools and universities are structured to mould pupils to be mostly obedient servants of rationality, and to develop outdated skills in interacting with outdated machines. We need to help our children learn how to best work with smart computers to improve human decision-making. But most of all we need to keep the long-term perspective in mind: that even if computers will outsmart us, we can still be the most creative. Because if we aren't, we won't be providing much value in future ecosystems, and that may put in question the foundation for our existence.", "55": "In a few decades, artificial intelligence (AI) will surpass many of the abilities that we believe make us special. This is a grand challenge for our age and it may require an \"irrational\" response.\nOne of the most significant pieces of news from the US in early 2017 was the efforts of Google to make autonomous driving a reality. According to a report, Google's self-driving cars clocked 1,023,330 km, and required human intervention 124 times. That is one intervention about every 8,047 km of autonomous driving. But even more impressive is the progress in just a single year: human interventions fell from 0.8 times per thousand miles to 0.2, a 400% improvement. With such progress, Google's cars will easily surpass my own driving ability later this year.\nDriving once seemed to be a very human skill. But we said that about chess, too. Then a computer beat the human world champion, repeatedly. The board game Go(围棋) took over from chess as a new test for human thinking in 2016, when a computer beat one of the world's leading professional Go players. With computers conquering what used to be deeply human tasks, what will it mean in the future to be human? I worry about my six-year-old son. What will his place be in a world where machines beat us in one area after another? He'll never calculate faster, never drive better, or even fly more safely. Actually, it all comes down to a fairly simple question: What's so special about us? It can't be skills like arithmetic, which machines already excel in. So far, machines have a pretty hard time emulating creativity, arbitrary enough not to be predicted by a computer, and yet more than simple randomness.\nPerhaps, if we continue to improve information-processing machines, we'll soon have helpful rational assistants. So we must aim to complement the rationality of the machine, rather than to compete with it. If I'm right, we should foster a creative spirit because a dose of illogical creativity will complement the rationality of the machine. Unfortunately, however, our education system has not caught up to the approaching reality. Indeed, our schools and universities are structured to mould pupils to be mostly obedient servants of rationality, and to develop outdated skills in interacting with outdated machines. We need to help our children learn how to best work with smart computers to improve human decision-making. But most of all we need to keep the long-term perspective in mind: that even if computers will outsmart us, we can still be the most creative. Because if we aren't, we won't be providing much value in future ecosystems, and that may put in question the foundation for our existence."}, "questions": [{"id": "2019-06-3-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abruptly", "B) additives", "C) approach", "D) ardently", "E) besieged", "F) channel", "G) comparable", "H) components", "I) cracked", "J) fractures", "K) hollow", "L) relevant", "M) reshuffled", "N) strived", "O) violent"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) abruptly\n副词\n【词性槽】实义动词前 / 助动词后修饰位\n【依据】钢在 -25℃ 以下\"突然\"变脆，与温度阈值语义吻合\nMost forms of steel __(26)__ become brittle(脆的) at temperatures below about\n-25℃：温度跨过一个临界点就发生状态突变，abruptly become brittle = 突然变脆，正贴\n临界温度的语义；同时也铺垫了后文\"低温脆性问题严重\"的迫切性。\n【竞争词】D) ardently ardently 是\"热切地\"，修饰人之情感\nardently 多与 love、support、admire 等表态度的动词搭配，主语通常是人；用它修饰\"钢变\n脆\"语义荒诞、感情色彩也完全错位。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·实义动词前 / 助动词后修饰位"]}, {"id": "2019-06-3-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abruptly", "B) additives", "C) approach", "D) ardently", "E) besieged", "F) channel", "G) comparable", "H) components", "I) cracked", "J) fractures", "K) hollow", "L) relevant", "M) reshuffled", "N) strived", "O) violent"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) fractures\n名词\n【词性槽】动词 resists 后需名词宾语\n【依据】与首句\"低温变脆\"承接，正是要抵抗的\"断裂\"\n首句已说 Most forms of steel __(26)__ become brittle(脆的) at temperatures below\nabout -25℃，brittle 的结果即断裂；新钢 resists __(27)__ at much lower temperatures\n抵抗的就是 fractures（断裂），上下衔接自然。\n【竞争词】H) components components 是\"部件\"，钢不会\"抵抗部件\"\nresist components 在语义上不成立；且 components 之归宿在第 34 空，作\"建筑所需的部\n件数\"。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·动词 resists 后需名词宾语"]}, {"id": "2019-06-3-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abruptly", "B) additives", "C) approach", "D) ardently", "E) besieged", "F) channel", "G) comparable", "H) components", "I) cracked", "J) fractures", "K) hollow", "L) relevant", "M) reshuffled", "N) strived", "O) violent"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) additives\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词 expensive 后缺名词宾语\n【依据】呼应上文\"加金属来抗脆\"的添加物语义\n上文 Most forms of steel __(26)__ become brittle(脆的) at temperatures below about\n-25℃ unless they are mixed with other metals. 已点明传统做法是加其他金属来抗脆，\n新钢则\"不用昂贵的添加物\"也能抗脆，additives 正是\"添加进去的东西\"，与 mixed with\nother metals 形成同义对应。\n【竞争词】H) components components 指\"组成部件\"而非\"添加物\"\ncomponents 常用于机器 / 结构的\"零件、组件\"，与\"为抗脆而往钢里加入的物质\"语义错位；且\ncomponents 真正用武之地在 They hope to reduce both the number of __(34)__ needed in\na construction job。\nJ) fractures fractures 是\"裂纹\"，与\"昂贵的\"搭不上\nfractures = 断裂 / 裂纹，是钢要避免的结果而不是\"昂贵的、被需要的东西\"，逻辑反；它另有\n归宿——填到第 27 空的 \"resists __\" 后面。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·形容词 expensive 后缺名词宾语"]}, {"id": "2019-06-3-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abruptly", "B) additives", "C) approach", "D) ardently", "E) besieged", "F) channel", "G) comparable", "H) components", "I) cracked", "J) fractures", "K) hollow", "L) relevant", "M) reshuffled", "N) strived", "O) violent"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) besieged\n形容词\n【词性槽】the + ___ + British 前作定语\n【依据】上文\"被 U 艇袭击\"正是\"被围困\"的同义改写\nAfter German U-boats torpedoed(用鱼雷攻击) numerous British ships, a 2700-strong\nfleet of cheap-and-cheerful \"Liberty ships\" was introduced to replace the lost\nvessels, providing a lifeline for the __(29)__ British. 给出\"船只被鱼雷击沉、急需救命\n船\"，besieged British = 处境艰难、被围困的英国人，与 lifeline（救命线）形成因果。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·the + ___ + British"]}, {"id": "2019-06-3-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abruptly", "B) additives", "C) approach", "D) ardently", "E) besieged", "F) channel", "G) comparable", "H) components", "I) cracked", "J) fractures", "K) hollow", "L) relevant", "M) reshuffled", "N) strived", "O) violent"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) cracked\n动词\n【词性槽】but 后从句需谓语动词\n【依据】钢壳在冰海里\"开裂\"，与紧接的 broke in half 顺承\nBut the steel shells of hundreds of the ships __(30)__ in the icy North Atlantic, and 12\nbroke in half and sank.：先开裂、后断成两半并沉没，cracked → broke → sank 是程度\n递进。\n【竞争词】N) strived strived 主语应是人，钢壳不会\"努力\"\nstrive = 努力奋斗，主语通常为人或群体，不能用于无生命的 steel shells；且与后面的\nbroke、sank 在语义上完全不连贯。它将在第 31 空大显身手。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·but 后从句需谓语动词"]}, {"id": "2019-06-3-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abruptly", "B) additives", "C) approach", "D) ardently", "E) besieged", "F) channel", "G) comparable", "H) components", "I) cracked", "J) fractures", "K) hollow", "L) relevant", "M) reshuffled", "N) strived", "O) violent"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) strived\n动词\n【词性槽】have ___ 现在完成时缺过去分词\n【依据】strive to do = 努力做某事，固定搭配\nSo scientists have strived to find a solution by mixing it with expensive metals such\nas nickel.：\"科学家一直努力通过加昂贵金属（如镍）来找解决办法\"，正是 strive to do 的\n标准用法，且与上句\"低温脆裂仍是问题\"形成因果。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·have ___ 现在完成时缺过去分词"]}, {"id": "2019-06-3-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abruptly", "B) additives", "C) approach", "D) ardently", "E) besieged", "F) channel", "G) comparable", "H) components", "I) cracked", "J) fractures", "K) hollow", "L) relevant", "M) reshuffled", "N) strived", "O) violent"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) approach\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词 physical 后缺名词中心词\n【依据】下句\"a complex mechanical process\"复指本词，即\"方法\"\n紧接的 Rather than adding other metals, they developed a complex mechanical\nprocess involving repeated heating and very severe mechanical deformation, known\nas temp forming. 是对前句\"more physical ___\"的同位展开，说明这是\"一种物理性的方法\n/ 路子\"，approach（方法 / 途径）正合。\n【竞争词】F) channel channel 偏\"渠道 / 海峡\"，不与 physical 搭配\nchannel 常指传播 / 沟通的渠道或地理上的海峡，与\"物理性的解决路子\"不搭；try a physical\nchannel 更不是固定表达。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 F) —— a physical channel 似通实错。考生可能误填 channel，因\n为 a more physical channel（更物理的渠道）字面读得过去；但后句 a complex\nmechanical process involving repeated heating and very severe mechanical\ndeformation, known as temp forming. 是\"方法 / 路子\"的同位解释，必须用 approach。\n防坑：读完空后再读一句\"是不是对前句的同义重申\"。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·形容词 physical 后缺名词中心词"]}, {"id": "2019-06-3-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abruptly", "B) additives", "C) approach", "D) ardently", "E) besieged", "F) channel", "G) comparable", "H) components", "I) cracked", "J) fractures", "K) hollow", "L) relevant", "M) reshuffled", "N) strived", "O) violent"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) comparable\n形容词\n【词性槽】be 动词 is 后需表语形容词\n【依据】新钢的强度韧性\"比得上\"昂贵合金钢，是全文卖点\n下句 that of modern steels that are very rich in alloy content and, therefore, very\nexpensive. 明确对照对象是\"含合金多、非常昂贵\"的现代钢，本文亮点正是新工艺钢\"性能\n可与之比肩\"，comparable to 即\"与…相当\"。\n【竞争词】L) relevant relevant 只是\"相关\"，落不下\"性能相当\"的强对比\nrelevant to = \"与…相关\"，说\"新钢的强度韧性与昂贵合金钢相关\"既不构成有意义的比较，也削\n弱了作者强调的\"以更低成本达到同等性能\"的卖点。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 L) —— be relevant to 误当成 be comparable to。两词都能与 to\n搭配，且 relevant 更眼熟，考生易凭眼熟选 L。防坑：a combination of strength and\ntoughness that is __(33)__ to that of modern steels that are very rich in alloy content\nand, therefore, very expensive. 的结构是性能间的\"比较\"而非\"相关\"，看到 that of ... 的\n对照结构就要用 comparable。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·be 动词 is 后需表语形容词"]}, {"id": "2019-06-3-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abruptly", "B) additives", "C) approach", "D) ardently", "E) besieged", "F) channel", "G) comparable", "H) components", "I) cracked", "J) fractures", "K) hollow", "L) relevant", "M) reshuffled", "N) strived", "O) violent"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) components\n名词\n【词性槽】the number of 后接可数名词复数\n【依据】建筑\"部件数\"是被减少的对象\n上文 make ultra-high strength parts, such as bolts. 已铺垫\"用作部件\"，空处 reduce\nboth the number of __(34)__ needed in a construction job and their weight 就是减少\n建筑中部件的数量与重量，components 完美对接 parts。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·the number of 后接可数名词"]}, {"id": "2019-06-3-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) abruptly", "B) additives", "C) approach", "D) ardently", "E) besieged", "F) channel", "G) comparable", "H) components", "I) cracked", "J) fractures", "K) hollow", "L) relevant", "M) reshuffled", "N) strived", "O) violent"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) hollow\n形容词\n【词性槽】名词 tubes 前缺定语形容词\n【依据】solid 实心 ↔ hollow 空心，反义对照天然\nreplacing solid supports with __(35)__ tubes 形成\"以…管代替实心支撑\"的替换关系；要\n在重量上更轻、对应上文 reduce their weight，自然要用 hollow（空心的）管子，与\nsolid 形成反义对照。\n【竞争词】E) besieged besieged 形容\"被围困\"，与钢管不搭\nbesieged 主语应是人 / 城市 / 国家（被围攻）；用来修饰工程用钢管语义错配，且与减重逻辑\n无关。\nO) violent violent tubes 语义荒诞\nviolent = 暴力的 / 猛烈的，与无生命的支撑钢管搭不上，也无法解释\"为何能替代实心支撑、减\n轻重量\"。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·名词 tubes 前缺定语形容词"]}, {"id": "2019-06-3-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Given the easier accessibility to space, it is time to think about how to prevent misuse of satellites.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】易得后防止误用\n【定位】CubeSat researchers suggest that now's the time to ponder unexpected and unintended\npossible consequences of more people than ever having access to their own small slice\nof space.\nCubeSat 研究者建议，眼下正是要思考这样一件事的时候：当比以往任何时候都更多的人能\n够拥有头顶这一小片太空时，可能带来什么意想不到、非本意的后果。\n【改写】it is time to think about how to prevent misuse of satellites ↔\nnow's the time to ponder unexpected and unintended possible consequences\n时间催促句+宾语上位\nGiven the easier accessibility to space ↔\nmore people than ever having access to their own small slice of space\n易得性的同义重写\n【辨邻】 B B 段的落点是把问题从能不能改写为该不该，是论域转换；Q36 强调此刻就要思考如\n何防止——动作和时间标记都更贴 now's the time to ponder\nThe question here is no longer \"Can we?\" but \"Should we?\"\nM M 段已经在谈 a culture of social responsibility around CubeSats becomes\nimportant 这样的解决路径与 a much wider public conversation，是承接呼吁后的\n对策章节；而 Q36 锁定的是初次发出此刻该思考误用的提醒，源头仍在 I 段\nThis is where a culture of social responsibility around CubeSats becomes important", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-06-3-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "A group of mini-satellites can work together to accomplish more complex tasks.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】迷你卫星组合完成复杂任务\n【定位】Multiples can be deployed in combination for more complex missions than could be achieved\nby one CubeSat alone.\n多颗 CubeSat 可以组合部署，去完成单颗 CubeSat 独自无法达成的更复杂任务。\n【改写】A group of mini-satellites ↔ Multiples can be deployed\n数量词同义\nwork together ↔ in combination\n协同方式同义\naccomplish more complex tasks ↔\nfor more complex missions than could be achieved by one CubeSat alone\n目标短语近义+对比补足", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-06-3-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "The greater accessibility of mini-satellites increases the risks of their irresponsible use.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】易得性提升不负责任使用风险\n【定位】However, as the cost of getting your own satellite in orbit drops sharply, the risks of\nirresponsible use grow.\n然而，随着把自家卫星送入轨道的成本大幅下降，被不负责任使用的风险也在增大。\n【改写】The greater accessibility of mini-satellites ↔\nas the cost of getting your own satellite in orbit drops sharply\n易得性与成本下降互为表里\nincreases the risks of their irresponsible use ↔ the risks of irresponsible use grow\n近乎逐词对应\n【辨邻】 I I 段确实呼应了风险话题，但用的是 more people than ever having access to their\nown small slice of space 来引出此刻该思考的号召；首次把 accessibility increases\nthe risks of irresponsible use 这层因果落到纸上的是 B 段，I 段更对应 Q36\nnow's the time to ponder unexpected and unintended possible consequences of more people than\never having access to their own small slice of space", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-06-3-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Even school pupils can have their CubeSats put in orbit owing to the lowered launching cost.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】小学生借成本下降把 CubeSat 送上轨\n【定位】This decrease in cost allows researchers, hobbyists and even elementary school groups to put\nsimple instruments into LEO or even have them deployed from the ISS.\n这种成本下降使研究人员、爱好者乃至小学生团体也能把简单的仪器送入低地球轨道，甚至\n从国际空间站释放。\n【改写】Even school pupils ↔ even elementary school groups\n用词不同的同义群体\nhave their CubeSats put in orbit ↔\nput simple instruments into LEO or even have them deployed from the ISS\n上位概括+具体场景\nowing to the lowered launching cost ↔ This decrease in cost allows\n因果指示词同义", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-06-3-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "AMSAT is careful about sharing information with outsiders to prevent hijacking of their satellites.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】AMSAT 对外谨慎以防劫持\n【定位】However, they're more cautious about sharing information with nonmembers, as the\norganization guards against others developing the ability to hijack and take control of\ntheir satellites.\n然而，他们在向非成员分享信息这件事上更为谨慎，因为该组织在防范他人获得劫持并接管\n其卫星的能力。\n【改写】AMSAT is careful about sharing information with outsiders ↔\nthey're more cautious about sharing information with nonmembers\n形近词+同义群体\nto prevent hijacking of their satellites ↔\nguards against others developing the ability to hijack and take control of their satellites\n防御性动词同源\n【辨邻】 J J 段说的是 making technical data on all aspects of their satellites fully available\nto everyone in the organization，是对内开源；而 sharing information with\noutsiders to prevent hijacking 的关键词在对外+防劫持，与 J 段方向相反，应配\nK 段\nAMSAT has a philosophy of open sourcing everything making technical data on all aspects of their\nsatellites fully available to everyone in the organization", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-06-3-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "NASA offers to launch CubeSats free of charge for educational and research purposes.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】NASA 免费发射教育与科研 CubeSat\n【定位】The NASA Educational Launch of Nano Satellite program, which offers free launches for\neducational groups and science missions, is now open to U.S. nonprofit corporations as well.\nNASA 的纳卫星教育发射计划为教育团体和科研任务提供免费发射，如今也对美国的非营利\n机构开放。\n【改写】NASA offers to launch CubeSats ↔\nThe NASA Educational Launch of Nano Satellite program, which offers free launches\n主体专名同形\nfree of charge ↔ free launches\n免费用语同义\nfor educational and research purposes ↔ for educational groups and science missions\n用途词同义", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-06-3-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Even with constraints, it is possible for some creative developers to take the CubeSat technology in directions that result in harmful outcomes.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】约束之下仍可能走向有害方向\n【定位】They're still constrained by funders, launch providers and a series of regulations—all of\nwhich rein in what CubeSat developers can and cannot do. But there's a danger they're ill-\nequipped to think through potential unintended consequences.\n他们仍然受到资助方、发射方和一系列法规的约束——这些都限制了 CubeSat 开发者能做\n什么、不能做什么。但是，他们可能没准备好去思考潜在的意外后果，这本身就是风险。\n【改写】Even with constraints ↔\nThey're still constrained by funders, launch providers and a series of regulations\n约束类型清单回收为名词\nsome creative developers to take the CubeSat technology in directions that result in harmful\noutcomes\n↔ innovators can be remarkably creative with taking technologies in unexpected directions\n走偏方向+创造性同义\n【辨邻】 I now's the time to ponder unexpected and unintended possible consequences\n强调的是该思考的时机，而 Q42 强调的是即便有约束仍可能走向有害方向；后者必须\n落在有明确约束清单+但仍可能的 L 段\nnow's the time to ponder unexpected and unintended possible consequences of more people than\never having access to their own small slice of space", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-06-3-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "While making significant contributions to space science, CubeSats may pose hazards to other space vehicles.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】重大贡献的同时威胁其他航天器\n【定位】Yet it also acknowledges that widespread deployment of LEO CubeSats isn't risk-free. The\ngreatest concern the authors raise is space debris—pieces of \"junk\" that orbit the earth, with\nthe potential to cause serious damage if they collide with operational units, including the ISS.\n但报告也承认，在低地球轨道大规模部署 CubeSat 并非毫无风险。作者最大的担忧是太空\n碎片——绕地球运行的垃圾，一旦与正在运行的设备（包括国际空间站）相撞，可能造成严\n重损坏。\n【改写】making significant contributions to space science ↔\nemphasizes CubeSats' importance in scientific discovery and the training of future space\nscientists and engineers\n贡献语同义改写\npose hazards to other space vehicles ↔\ncause serious damage if they collide with operational units, including the ISS\n威胁词+对象上位\n【辨邻】 H H 段确实谈论威胁，但接着 near-misses 谈 creation of a burdensome regulatory\nframework，重点落在监管后果而非首次把贡献与威胁并列；Q43 的 while 句式要求\n贡献与威胁并列出现，这只在 G 段同时具备\neven near-misses might lead to the \"creation of a burdensome regulatory framework and affect the\nfuture disposition of science CubeSats\"", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-06-3-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Mini-satellites enable operators to study Earth from LEO and space around it.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】在 LEO 同时研究地球与周边空间\n【定位】Within their compact bodies these minute satellites are able to house sensors and\ncommunications receivers/transmitters that enable operators to study Earth from space, as\nwell as space around Earth.\n在迷你身躯之内，这些微型卫星能够容纳传感器以及通信收发设备，使操作者得以从太空研\n究地球，同时也研究地球周边的空间。\n【改写】Mini-satellites enable operators ↔\nthese minute satellites are able to house sensors and communications receivers/transmitters\nthat enable operators\n主体词+动词同义\nto study Earth from LEO and space around it ↔\nto study Earth from space, as well as space around Earth\n并列宾语近乎逐字", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-06-3-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "AMSAT operates on the principle of having all its technical data accessible to its members, preventing the abuse of amateur satellites.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】AMSAT 内部数据全开放防滥用\n【定位】Within the organization, AMSAT has a philosophy of open sourcing everything making\ntechnical data on all aspects of their satellites fully available to everyone in the organization,\nand when possible, the public. According to a member of the team responsible for FOX 1-\nA, AMSAT's first CubeSat, this means that there's no way to sneak something like explosives\nor an energy emitter into an amateur satellite when everyone has access to the designs\nand implementation.\n在组织内部，AMSAT 奉行将一切开源的理念，把卫星各方面的技术数据完全向组织内每一\n个人开放，并在可能时面向公众。据 AMSAT 第一颗 CubeSat（FOX 1-A）负责团队的成员\n介绍，这意味着——只要所有人都能接触到设计和实现，就没有办法偷偷把炸药或能量发射\n器这类东西塞进一颗业余卫星里。\n【改写】AMSAT operates on the principle ↔ AMSAT has a philosophy of open sourcing everything\n原则与理念同义\nhaving all its technical data accessible to its members ↔\nmaking technical data on all aspects of their satellites fully available to everyone in the\norganization\n近乎逐字对应\npreventing the abuse of amateur satellites ↔\nthere's no way to sneak something like explosives or an energy emitter into an amateur\nsatellite when everyone has access to the designs and implementation\n防滥用的具体场景上位概括\n【辨邻】 K more cautious about sharing information with nonmembers 与 guards against\nothers developing the ability to hijack and take control of their satellites 强调的\n是对非成员不分享、防被劫持，而 Q45 的关键是 accessible to its members +\npreventing abuse，源头段必须是对内全面开放的 J 段\nthey're more cautious about sharing information with nonmembers, as the organization guards\nagainst others developing the ability to hijack and take control of their satellites\nPARAGRAPH MAP · 段落主旨图\n个段落各讲了什么\n逐段一句主旨 + 该段最具区分度的「信息指纹」，是逐句回扫定位的工作台。\nA\n卫星过去是富国和大公司的专属玩具，如今随着太空民主化，正像无人机一样走入普\n通人手中，根本性地改变了头顶上方是谁在做什么的格局。\n信息指纹 无人机类比+太空民主化\nB\n国家科学院报告指出微型卫星让卫星科学前所未有地普及，但成本暴跌也带来不负责\n任使用的风险，问题已从能不能变成该不该。\n信息指纹 可不可以变该不该\nC\n介绍 CubeSat 这一独特类别：边长 10 厘米的立方体，可塞进运载火箭过去被浪费的\n空间，多颗组合能完成单颗做不到的复杂任务。\n信息指纹 10 厘米立方体+组合发射\nD 迷你卫星体内装有传感器和收发机，主要在距地面 200 到 800 英里的低地球轨道运\n行，既可研究地球本身、也可研究地球周边空间，未来还可能跑得更远。\n信息指纹 在 LEO 同时研究地球与周边空间\nE 正因为小而轻，CubeSat 入轨成本远低于传统卫星，连研究人员、爱好者乃至小学生\n团体都用得起，亚利桑那州立大学的项目甚至声称只需三千美元。\n信息指纹 三千美元+小学生也能上天\nF 回溯 CubeSat 起源：本世纪初由斯坦福研究生发起，如今 NASA、国家侦察办公\n室、波音都在运行 CubeSat，且 NASA 的纳卫星教育发射计划免费向教育与科研团体\n开放。\n信息指纹 NASA 免费发射教育卫星\nG 国家科学院报告强调 CubeSat 对科学发现和培养新一代航天人才的重要意义，但同\n时承认在低地球轨道大规模部署并非零风险，最担心的是与 ISS 等可能相撞的太空\n碎片。\n信息指纹 贡献伴随太空碎片威胁\nH\n目前 CubeSat 数量不多、能被密切追踪，但随着低地球轨道开放，威胁可能变大，连\n险些相撞都可能催生繁琐的监管框架，反过来影响科研 CubeSat 的未来。\n信息指纹 险些相撞催生监管\nI\nCubeSat 研究者呼吁，正是因为购买套件就能上手，现在该认真思考更多人涉足太空\n可能带来的意外后果，要确保头顶上的卫星出自怀有好意且懂行的人之手。\n信息指纹 易得后思考误用风险\nJ\n介绍 AMSAT：1969 年成立，承袭 1961 年 Project OSCAR 的努力；作为志愿者组\n织，几十年来在组织内部全面开源所有技术资料，从而防止有人偷偷往业余卫星里塞\n炸药或能量发射器。\n信息指纹 AMSAT 内部全面开源防滥用\nK AMSAT 对非成员则更为谨慎，警惕外界获取信息后劫持并接管自家卫星；这种自治在\n老牌业余组织里行得通，但新玩家入场时未必。\n信息指纹 对外谨慎防劫持\nL 爱好者和学生在未加入老牌业余组织的情况下就能拿到技术，虽受到资助方、发射方\n和监管的约束，但仍可能把技术带向意想不到的方向；以手机为例，从普惠金融到简\n易爆炸装置两端皆有。\n信息指纹 受约束仍可能走偏的手机类比\nM\n因此围绕 CubeSat 的社会责任文化十分重要，不只是降低物理风险，还要在更大社区\n里预见和管理不那么显眼的后果；需要一场把学生、爱好者和潜在受影响者也包括进\n来的更广泛公共对话。\n信息指纹 更广泛公共对话", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-06-3-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What did the author realize when he re-entered the corporate world?", "options": ["A) Making new friends with his workmates was not as easy as he had anticipated.", "B) Cultivating positive interpersonal relationships helped him expel solitary feelings.", "C) Working in the corporate world requires more interpersonal skills than self-employment.", "D) Building close relationships with his colleagues was not as important as he had expected."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What did the author realize 是对作者具体所”想到”内容的直接发问，没有 infer、imply\n之类的推断信号，答案应在原文里有近乎对应的句子。\n【拆句】Q46 是标准 wh-疑问句：主语 the author、谓语 realize，when 引导时间状语从句 when\nhe re-entered the corporate world。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】It wasn't until I entered the corporate world that I realized, for me at least, being friends with\ncolleagues didn't emerge as a priority at all.\n直到我重回公司职场我才意识到，至少对我而言，和同事做朋友这件事根本算不上什么优\n先项。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nMaking new friends with his workmates was not as easy as he had anticipated 说\n的是”交朋友没那么容易”，原文 didn't emerge as a priority at all 说的是”根本不是优\n先项 = 不重要”；“难易”和”重要不重要”是两码事，属于偷换概念。\nB) 与原文相反\nCultivating positive interpersonal relationships helped him expel solitary feelings\n表态是”积极关系帮他赶走了孤独”，与原文”作者发现其实没那么重要、连期待都落空了”\n的语义方向相反；且原文从未说同事关系帮他驱散孤独。\nC) 无中生有\nWorking in the corporate world requires more interpersonal skills than self-\nemployment 把公司职场与自雇放在”人际技能要求”上做比较，原文只说作者”再次进入\n公司发现交朋友不重要”，从未把两种工作模式的人际技能要求拿来比较。\nD) ✓ 正确\nbeing friends with colleagues didn't emerge as a priority at all ↔\nBuilding close relationships with his colleagues was not as important as he had expected.\nthere was one thing I was looking forward to the most ↔ as he had expected\n预判说”和同事做朋友不像他原以为的那样重要”，D 把”和同事做朋友”改写为 Building\nclose relationships with his colleagues，把”不重要”改写为 was not as important as\nhe had expected，两端语义完全对应。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-3-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What do we learn from many studies about collegial relationships?", "options": ["A) Inharmonious relationships have an adverse effect on productivity.", "B) Harmonious relationships are what many companies aim to cultivate.", "C) Close collegial relationships contribute very little to product quality.", "D) Conflicting relationships in the workplace exist almost everywhere."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn from many studies 询问已有研究的具体发现，是对客观信息的复述，\n归入细节题。\n【拆句】Q47 主干 What do we learn，from 引导信息来源 from many studies，about 引导话题\nabout collegial relationships。无从句、无否定，无理解陷阱。\n【定位】So much research has explored the way in which collegial(同事的) ties can help overcome a\nrange of workplace issues affecting productivity and the quality of work output such as team-\nbased conflict, jealousy, undermining, anger, and more.\n大量研究已经探究了同事间的关系如何帮助化解一系列影响生产率与工作产出质量的职场问\n题，比如团队冲突、嫉妒、暗中拆台、愤怒等等。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\ncollegial(同事的) ties can help overcome a range of workplace issues affecting productivity and\nthe quality of work output such as team-based conflict, jealousy, undermining, anger, and more\n↔ Inharmonious relationships have an adverse effect on productivity.\nteam-based conflict, jealousy, undermining, anger ↔ Inharmonious relationships\n预判说”和谐关系能化解影响生产率的问题”，等价于”关系不和谐就会损害生产率”，正\n与 Inharmonious relationships have an adverse effect on productivity 在逻辑上\n对应。\nB) 偷换概念\nHarmonious relationships are what many companies aim to cultivate 谈的是”公司\n想培育”，原文谈的是”研究探究关系如何化解职场问题”；研究发现 ≠ 公司目标，属于偷\n换概念。\nC) 与原文相反\nClose collegial relationships contribute very little to product quality 说”对产品质\n量贡献很小”，原文 can help overcome a range of workplace issues affecting\nproductivity and the quality of work output 说的是”能帮助克服影响产出质量的问\n题”，恰好相反。\nD) 过度推断\nConflicting relationships in the workplace exist almost everywhere 把原文里仅作\n为”被研究的负面问题之一”提到的 conflict 放大成”在职场几乎到处存在”的全称断言，超\n出原文范围。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-3-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What can be inferred about relationships at work from an academic analysis?", "options": ["A) They should be cultivated.", "B) They are virtually irrelevant.", "C) They are vital to corporate culture.", "D) They should be reasonably intimate."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （推断题）\n【判型】What can be inferred 直接给出 infer 字样，按规则归为推断题，需要从定位句做一步合理\n延伸。\n【拆句】Q48 主干 What can be inferred about relationships at work，from 引出依据 from an\nacademic analysis。被动 can be inferred 等于 we can infer，理解上无陷阱。\n【定位】It's a simple term that encapsulates(概括) the fact that relationships at work can reasonably be\nnon-intimate, inconsequential, unimportant and even, dare I say it, disposable or substitutable.\n这是个简单的术语，它概括了一个事实：职场关系完全可以是非亲密的、无足轻重的、不重\n要的，甚至——我冒昧地说——可有可无、可被替代。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nThey should be cultivated 表达”应当被培育”，与原文该学术分析对职场关系”非亲密、\n不重要、可替代”的去价值化描述方向相反。\nB) ✓ 正确\nnon-intimate, inconsequential, unimportant and even, dare I say it, disposable or substitutable ↔\nThey are virtually irrelevant.\n预判”职场关系无关紧要”，They are virtually irrelevant 用 virtually irrelevant 概括五\n个形容词的共同语义场，正是这一步推断的最佳落点。\nC) 无中生有\nThey are vital to corporate culture 谈”对企业文化至关重要”，但原文该学术分析既未\n提 corporate culture，也未把职场关系定性为 vital，且与 unimportant 直接矛盾。\nD) 偷换概念\nThey should be reasonably intimate 主张”应该相当亲密”，与原文 can reasonably\nbe non-intimate 中 reasonably 修饰 non-intimate（合理地非亲密）相反；干扰项保\n留了原文的 reasonably 这个词，但把 non-intimate 偷成了 intimate。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-3-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What does the author say about people who are socially lazy?", "options": ["A) They feel Uncomfortable when engaging in social interactions.", "B) They often find themselves in confrontation with their colleagues.", "C) They are Unwilling to make efforts to maintain Workplace relationships.", "D) They lack basic communication skills in dealing with interpersonal issues."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say about people who are socially lazy 直接问作者对某群体的\n具体说法，是对原文一句话的同义复述，归入细节题。\n【拆句】Q49 主干 What does the author say about people，who are socially lazy 是限定\npeople 的定语从句。结构清晰、无否定，无理解陷阱。\n【定位】Indifference is also the preferred option among those who are socially lazy. Maintaining\nrelationships over the long term takes effort. For some of us, too much effort.\n对那些”社交懒人”来说，冷漠（无关紧要型关系）也是首选。维系长期关系需要付出努力\n——而对我们中的一些人而言，付出得太多了。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nThey feel Uncomfortable when engaging in social interactions 谈”社交时感到不\n适”，原文只提到”社交懒人”嫌维系关系费力，从未谈他们参与社交时的心理感受。\nB) 张冠李戴\nThey often find themselves in confrontation with their colleagues 把”冲突”赋给”社\n交懒人”，但原文 harmony over confrontation 是描述”看重独立、追求和谐”的人偏好\n冷漠关系，与”社交懒人”是并列的两类，把另一群体的属性套到当前题问的群体头上。\nC) ✓ 正确\nMaintaining relationships over the long term takes effort. For some of us, too much effort. ↔\nThey are Unwilling to make efforts to maintain Workplace relationships.\nIndifference is also the preferred option among those who are socially lazy ↔ They\n预判”嫌维护关系太费力”，Unwilling to make efforts to maintain Workplace\nrelationships 把”嫌费力”明示为”不愿付出努力维护职场关系”，正是预判的同义改写。\nD) 无中生有\nThey lack basic communication skills in dealing with interpersonal issues 谈”缺乏\n基本沟通技能”，原文从未评价”社交懒人”的沟通能力，只说他们”嫌费力”。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-3-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What is one of the benefits of indifferent relationships?", "options": ["A) They provide fun at work.", "B) They help control emotions.", "C) They help resolve differences.", "D) They improve Work efficiency."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What is one of the benefits of indifferent relationships 是对原文已列出的好处之一的直\n接发问，无推断信号，归入细节题。\n【拆句】Q50 主干 What is one of the benefits，of indifferent relationships 是限定 benefits 的\n介词短语。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】One of those is efficiency. Less time chatting and socializing means more time working and\nchurning(产出).\n其中一项是效率。少花时间闲聊和社交就意味着更多时间用在工作和产出上。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nThey provide fun at work 说”提供工作乐趣”，原文 None of that might be as fun as\nafter-work socializing 明示”无关紧要型关系的这些好处可能并不如下班后社交那么有\n趣”，与”提供乐趣”方向相反。\nB) 偷换概念\nThey help control emotions 说”帮助控制情绪”，原文 the emotional neutrality of\nindifferent relationships 说的是”无关紧要型关系本身在情感上是中性的”——是结果而\n非手段，且没有”控制情绪”的意思。\nC) 与原文相反\nThey help resolve differences 说”帮助化解分歧”，原文 As noted above, indifferent\nrelationships may not always be the most helpful approach in resolving some of\nthe issues that pop up at work 恰好提示”无关紧要型关系并不总是解决问题的最好方\n式”——化解问题不是它的好处。\nD) ✓ 正确\nOne of those is efficiency. Less time chatting and socializing means more time working and\nchurning(产出).\n↔ They improve Work efficiency.\n预判”提升工作效率”，They improve Work efficiency 把这一好处直陈出来，与定位句\n一一对应，是首段并列项里最显眼的一条。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-3-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What is the author's greatest concern about the use of AI?", "options": ["A) Computers are performing lots of creative tasks.", "B) Many abilities will cease to be unique to human beings.", "C) Computers may become more rational than humans.", "D) Many human skills are fast becoming outdated."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What is the author's greatest concern about the use of AI? 问的是作者最担心的具体一\n件事，对应原文某处明示，不需多步推理。\n【拆句】What is the author's greatest concern about the use of AI? 主干 What is the author's\nconcern，about the use of AI 介词短语作 concern 的限定语；greatest 形容词最高级修\n饰 concern，强调”最”——只能选一个最大担忧。\n【定位】I worry about my six-year-old son. What will his place be in a world where machines beat\nus in one area after another? He'll never calculate faster, never drive better, or even fly\nmore safely.\n我担忧我六岁的儿子。在一个机器在一个又一个领域击败我们的世界里，他的位置何在？他\n永远不会算得更快，永远不会开得更好，甚至飞得更安全。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nComputers are performing lots of creative tasks 称计算机正在完成大量创造性任\n务，与 machines have a pretty hard time emulating creativity 直接相反——原文恰\n恰强调机器在创造性上做得很差。\nB) ✓ 正确\nartificial intelligence (AI) will surpass many of the abilities that we believe make us special ↔\nMany abilities will cease to be unique to human beings\nWhat will his place be in a world where machines beat us in one area after another? ↔\nMany abilities will cease to be unique to human beings\n预判”人类独特能力将不再独特”，Many abilities will cease to be unique to human\nbeings 是该预判的标准转述，主语 abilities 与谓语 cease to be unique 与原文\nsurpass/beat us 完全等值。\nC) 无中生有\nComputers may become more rational than humans 谈”计算机比人更理性”这一比\n较，原文 we must aim to complement the rationality of the machine 只承认机器理\n性、号召人去补足它，从未做”比人更理性”的比较，更没把它说成作者担忧点。\nD) 答非所问\nMany human skills are fast becoming outdated 取自 to develop outdated skills in\ninteracting with outdated machines，原文 outdated 用于批评教育体系仍训练过时\n技能，并非作者最大担忧；最大担忧是独特性丧失，不是技能过时。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-3-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What impresses the author most in the field of AI?", "options": ["A) Google's experimental driverless cars require little human intervention.", "B) Google's cars have surpassed his driving ability in just a single year.", "C) Google has made huge progress in autonomous driving in a short time.", "D) Google has become a world leader in the field of autonomous driving."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What impresses the author most in the field of AI? 问作者最印象深的具体一件事，原文\n应有明示的”最”标记词。\n【拆句】What impresses the author most in the field of AI? 主干 What impresses the\nauthor，most 副词修饰动词 impresses，强调最高程度；in the field of AI 介词短语限定\n范围。\n【定位】But even more impressive is the progress in just a single year: human interventions fell from\n0.8 times per thousand miles to 0.2, a 400% improvement.\n但更令人印象深刻的是仅仅一年内的进步：人为干预从每千英里 0.8 次降到 0.2 次，进步\n达 400%。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\nGoogle's experimental driverless cars require little human intervention 描述的是\nrequired human intervention 124 times. That is one intervention about every\n8,047 km 的事实，原文确有，但作者并未用 most impressive 评价它——most\nimpressive 留给了”一年内的进步幅度”。\nB) 偷换概念\nGoogle's cars have surpassed his driving ability in just a single year 把”一年内有\n400% 进步”偷换为”一年内已超过作者驾驶水平”，但原文 Google's cars will easily\nsurpass my own driving ability later this year 用的是将来时 will...later this year——\n尚未超越，且不是 most impressive 的对象。\nC) ✓ 正确\neven more impressive is the progress in just a single year ↔\nGoogle has made huge progress in autonomous driving in a short time\nhuman interventions fell from 0.8 times per thousand miles to 0.2, a 400% improvement ↔\nGoogle has made huge progress in autonomous driving in a short time\n预判”短时间内取得巨大进步”，Google has made huge progress in autonomous\ndriving in a short time 双双匹配 huge=400% improvement、in a short time=in\njust a single year，完整覆盖。\nD) 无中生有\nGoogle has become a world leader in the field of autonomous driving 给谷歌贴”\n世界领导者”标签，原文从未做这种排位评价，仅给出谷歌自身的进度数据。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-3-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What do we learn from the passage about creativity?", "options": ["A) It is rational.", "B) It is predictable.", "C) It is human specific.", "D) It is yet to be emulated by AI."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn from the passage about creativity? 是问原文对 creativity 这一概念\n描述了什么，答案需直接对应原文表述。\n【拆句】What do we learn from the passage about creativity? 主干 What do we learn；from\nthe passage 状语指明信息来源；about creativity 介词短语限定信息话题。\n【定位】So far, machines have a pretty hard time emulating creativity, arbitrary enough not to be\npredicted by a computer, and yet more than simple randomness.\n迄今为止，机器很难模拟创造力——创造力足够任意以致于无法被计算机预测，但又不只是\n简单的随机。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nIt is rational 说创造力是理性的，但原文 a dose of illogical creativity will\ncomplement the rationality of the machine 用 illogical 形容 creativity，并把它与\nrationality 对立——创造力恰恰是非理性。\nB) 与原文相反\nIt is predictable 称创造力可预测，原文 arbitrary enough not to be predicted by a\ncomputer 明示创造力”任意到不能被预测”——选项直接翻转。\nC) 过度推断\nIt is human specific 把创造力升级为”人类专属”，原文只说”机器目前难以模拟创造\n力”，并未排除其它生物或未来机器也可能有，”专属”是越过原文的强判断。\nD) ✓ 正确\nmachines have a pretty hard time emulating creativity ↔ It is yet to be emulated by AI\n预判”机器尚未能模拟创造力”，It is yet to be emulated by AI 主谓结构与原文完全等\n值；yet to be 表”尚未发生”，对应原文 have a hard time + So far 的语义。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-3-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What should schools help children do in the era of AI?", "options": ["A) Cultivate original thinking.", "B) Learn to work independently.", "C) Compete with smart machines.", "D) Understand how AI works."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What should schools help children do in the era of AI? 问学校应做什么，原文必有\nshould/need to/must 的建议句直接对应。\n【拆句】What should schools help children do in the era of AI? 主干 What should schools\nhelp children do；help children do 为”help sb. do”的省略 to 不定式结构；in the era of\nAI 状语限定时代背景。\n【定位】If I'm right, we should foster a creative spirit because a dose of illogical creativity will\ncomplement the rationality of the machine.\n如果我说得对，我们应当培育一种创造性精神，因为一剂非逻辑的创造力将补足机器的\n理性。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nwe should foster a creative spirit ↔ Cultivate original thinking\nwe can still be the most creative ↔ Cultivate original thinking\n预判”培育创造性精神”，Cultivate original thinking 在动词（cultivate=foster）与名\n词短语（original thinking=creative spirit）两层做同义替换，是标准答案。\nB) 无中生有\nLearn to work independently 提”独立工作”，原文从未提及；恰相反，原文 learn\nhow to best work with smart computers 强调与机器协作，而非独立。\nC) 与原文相反\nCompete with smart machines 说与机器竞争，原文 we must aim to complement\nthe rationality of the machine, rather than to compete with it 明确反对竞争、主张\n互补——选项直接翻转主张。\nD) 偷换概念\nUnderstand how AI works 说理解 AI 的工作原理，原文谈的是”如何与智能计算机协作\n以改善决策”，并非”理解 AI 工作原理”——它是相邻语境的概念嫁接，非作者主张。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-06-3-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "How can we humans justify our future existence?", "options": ["A) By constantly outsmarting computers.", "B) By adopting a long-term perspective.", "C) By rationally compromising with AI.", "D) By providing value with our creativity."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】How can we humans justify our future existence? 问”如何”，对应原文某句给出的具体\n路径，可逐字落地。\n【拆句】How can we humans justify our future existence? 主干 How can we justify our\nexistence；humans 与 we 是同位语，强调”我们人类”；future 形容词修饰 existence。\n【定位】Because if we aren't, we won't be providing much value in future ecosystems, and that may\nput in question the foundation for our existence.\n因为如果我们不是（最有创造力的），我们将无法在未来的生态中提供太多价值，而这可能\n会让我们存在的根基受到质疑。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nBy constantly outsmarting computers 称要”持续比计算机更聪明”，原文 even if\ncomputers will outsmart us 已让步承认 computers will outsmart us（计算机将更聪\n明），人类靠的是创造力而非更聪明——选项直接反转。\nB) 答非所问\nBy adopting a long-term perspective 取自 we need to keep the long-term\nperspective in mind，原文是”在心中保持长远视角”作为提醒方式，并非作者给出的”为\n存在正名”的根本手段——根本手段是创造力。\nC) 无中生有\nBy rationally compromising with AI 称”与 AI 理性妥协”，原文从未出现 compromise\n或同义词，原文主张是”互补”（complement），不是妥协。\nD) ✓ 正确\nif we aren't, we won't be providing much value in future ecosystems ↔\nBy providing value with our creativity\neven if computers will outsmart us, we can still be the most creative ↔\nBy providing value with our creativity\n预判”靠创造力提供价值”，By providing value with our creativity 双双匹配 creativity\n（手段）与 providing value（目的），且 by 短语正是 how 题的标准回答形式。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}], "listeningSharedWith": "2019-06-1"});
